<section class="section section--tight" aria-labelledby="day-title">
  <div class="wrap two-col">
    <div>
      <h2 id="day-title">Decide these in the first month</h2>
      <ol class="mini-steps test-steps">
        <li>Entity choice, with its yearly compliance. See {% routeLink "/services/company-llp-registration/", "company and LLP registration" %}</li>
        <li>A business bank account in the entity's name</li>
        <li>Cloud accounting with your own login and a chart of accounts</li>
        <li>How founder money going in is treated: as share capital or a loan, documented</li>
        <li>How expenses paid personally get reimbursed</li>
        <li>One folder for all agreements and equity documents</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>Founder expenses: the classic mess</h2>
      <p>Founders commonly pay early costs on personal cards. Before and after incorporation need separate thought.</p>
      <dl class="fit-list">
        <div><dt>Before incorporation</dt><dd>Keep every invoice. Ask your adviser how these costs can be taken into the company.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>After incorporation</dt><dd>Use a simple monthly claim with invoices attached. Ask for invoices in the company's name and GSTIN where possible.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Money the founder puts in</dt><dd>Record it as a loan or capital with a paper trail. Lending rules for companies have conditions, so check with a professional.</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--tint" aria-labelledby="dd-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="section-head">
      <h2 id="dd-title">What a diligence request often looks for</h2>
      <p>This is our general picture of a typical request, not any particular investor's list. Keeping these up to date is far cheaper than recreating them.</p>
    </header>
    <section class="data-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Diligence checklist, scrolls sideways on small screens">
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Area</th><th scope="col">What to have ready</th><th scope="col">Start from</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Financials</th><td>Monthly accounts, audited statements where required, and reconciled bank</td><td>Month one</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Tax</th><td>GST and TDS returns, with challans and any notices</td><td>From registration</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Company law</th><td>Board minutes, registers, MCA filings, and share issue records</td><td>From incorporation</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Equity</th><td>Cap table, investor agreements, ESOP documents</td><td>First share issue</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">People</th><td>Contracts, payroll and PF or ESI where applicable</td><td>First hire</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Customers and IP</th><td>Key contracts, and evidence that the company owns its IP</td><td>First contract</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" aria-labelledby="report-title">
  <div class="wrap layout-side">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2 id="report-title" class="first-h2">A monthly report that fits on one page</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Cash at bank</strong> and <strong>monthly burn</strong>, gross and net</li>
        <li><strong>Runway:</strong> cash divided by net burn</li>
        <li><strong>Revenue,</strong> with what is recurring and what is one-off</li>
        <li><strong>Actual against budget</strong> for the top few cost lines</li>
        <li><strong>Payments due:</strong> GST, TDS, salaries and big vendor payments</li>
      </ul>
      <p>If you outgrow this, it may be time for {% routeLink "/services/virtual-cfo/", "virtual CFO support" %}, though many early companies do not need it yet.</p>

      <h2 id="traps">Traps specific to startups</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Assuming "startup" means no compliance.</strong> A company has audit and filing duties from its first year, even without revenue.</li>
        <li><strong>Treating DPIIT recognition as automatic relief.</strong> Any benefit has its own conditions, so check at source.</li>
        <li><strong>Issuing shares without proper process or valuation.</strong> Take professional advice before you raise money.</li>
        <li><strong>Paying contractors with no TDS check.</strong> Check whether TDS applies.</li>
        <li><strong>Foreign money or payments.</strong> Foreign investment and payments abroad have their own rules. Get advice early.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="ask">What to ask an accountant</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Have you worked with funded or pre-funding startups?</li>
        <li>Can you prepare monthly accounts, not only yearly ones?</li>
        <li>Who handles MCA filings and board records?</li>
        <li>Will you tell me what an investor would question in my books today?</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="note">This page gives no investment, valuation or fundraising advice. Those matters need a qualified professional.</p>
    </div>
